Compassion Fatigue in Community Advocacy - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Occupational problem)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Emotional numbness, Hopelessness regarding systemic change, Avoidance of community news

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-008
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of intractable vague gastrointestinal distress, severe irritability, and chronic physical exhaustion mimicking an acute intra-abdominal process.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, unremitting nausea, vague abdominal pain, and extreme fatigue over 48 hours, necessitating urgent physical assessment to exclude an acute surgical abdomen or severe dehydration before routine GP hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel sick to my stomach constantly, I can't sleep, and I honestly don't care about anything anymore.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of emotional numbness and an inability to generate empathy for new crises, alongside a pervasive sense of hopelessness regarding systemic change. Over the past 48 hours, the patient has developed somatic complaints including vague gastrointestinal distress (nausea, cramping) and severe tension headaches. The spouse reports the patient exhibits avoidance of community news, cynical and dark humor regarding traumatic events, and irritability directed at allies and co-workers. The patient notes chronic physical exhaustion unbroken by standard rest, decreased effectiveness in advocacy tasks, and feelings of intense guilt for taking personal time off.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired by exhaustion and gastrointestinal discomfort; unable to perform occupational or personal duties.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of occupational burnout and relational strain due to extreme irritability.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Appears exhausted, irritable, and restless. Eye contact is poor, and affect is notably flat and detached.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, normal heart sounds. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Abdomen: Soft, non-distended. Mild, diffuse tenderness to deep palpation across all quadrants without guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness. Bowel sounds are active. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Emotional numbness and avoidance of community news are central to the clinical presentation; the patient actively asked the spouse to turn off a news radio broadcast upon the clinician's arrival.
Environmental Observations: Several smartphones and a laptop are piled on a desk, physically turned face down or closed.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescribed medications. Taking over-the-counter antacids with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Compassion Fatigue in Community Advocacy - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Occupational problem) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis / Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lack of focal, severe abdominal pain, absence of peritonism (guarding/rebound), and no fever.

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary etiology is directly occupational and trauma-exposure related, although symptoms overlap; the specific anhedonia is localized to advocacy and community events rather than global.

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of acute vomiting, diarrhea, or fever, and the chronicity of the underlying fatigue.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, functionally limiting gastrointestinal distress, somatic pain, and extreme fatigue in the context of chronic high-stakes community advocacy. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was required to definitively palpate the abdomen and exclude an acute surgical emergency (such as appendicitis) or severe organic pathology driving the systemic symptoms. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a risk of missed acute abdominal pathology and would have perpetuated severe physical and psychological distress, firmly satisfying the criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral metoclopramide 10mg as needed for nausea to restore oral intake. Recommended the usual GP order psychometric indicators of secondary traumatic stress and assess heart rate variability.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage emotional numbness and avoidance, initiate a "digital quarantine" for 48 hours, physically removing access to all news and advocacy platforms; substituting this with a low-stakes, tactile activity (e.g., gardening, baking) will redirect cognitive focus and reduce sympathetic tone. To address guilt regarding rest, schedule a 15-minute daily window for "mandatory inaction" (sitting quietly without stimuli); labeling this as a prescribed medical intervention will help bypass the guilt associated with voluntary rest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to occupational psychology specializing in secondary trauma.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 3 days for abdominal exam reassessment and referral coordination.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if developing severe, sharp abdominal pain, persistent vomiting preventing fluid intake, vomiting blood, or developing a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic and require ongoing review.
Clinical Course: The acute gastrointestinal symptoms should settle with antiemetics and significant reduction in psychological stress. Recovery from compassion fatigue requires prolonged, structured time away from the traumatic stimuli.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if your stomach pain suddenly becomes sharp and unbearable, if your stomach feels hard like a board, or if you cannot keep any water down for more than 12 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The continuous exposure to high-stress, traumatic information has shifted your nervous system into a chronic 'fight or flight' state, which directly shuts down normal digestion and causes nausea. The medication will settle your stomach, but true recovery requires stopping the input of stress.
Actionable Steps: Take the anti-nausea medication 30 minutes before meals. Strictly adhere to your 48-hour digital quarantine to allow your nervous system to reset.

